  "sport": "basketball",
  "title": "Ball raiders – dribble and shield",
  "description": "Everyone stays inside a half court (full court if the group is big). About two out of three players have a ball and dribble freely. The rest are raiders without a ball who try to poke away or steal a ball from someone who is dribbling.\n\nIf a raider wins a ball, they keep it and start dribbling, and the player who lost it becomes a new raider. A new raider may not go straight back after the player who just took the ball. No grabbing or slapping arms – that is a foul.\n\nVary it: more raiders means higher tempo and more pressure, a smaller area means more traffic. Progression: ask players to try crossovers between the legs or behind the back when a raider comes.",
  "coachingPoints": [
    {
      "id": "cp1",
      "text": "Eyes up – spot the raiders before they arrive."
    },
    {
      "id": "cp2",
      "text": "Keep dribbling when it gets crowded. Do not pick the ball up (double dribble)."
    },
    {
      "id": "cp3",
      "text": "Shield the ball with your body and free arm, without pushing."
    },
    {
      "id": "cp4",
      "text": "Raiders: go for the ball, not the arm."
    }
  ],
  "locale": "en",
  "phase": "warmup",
  "difficulty": 1,
